   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the 2023/24 FY, DFAT entered a grant arrangement with the Pacific Community (SPC), to establish the Pacific Humanitarian Warehousing Program (PHWP). PHWP will support 14 Pacific island countries and Timor-Leste, to acquire a sovereign, humanitarian warehousing capability to improve their disaster preparedness and resilience. Although still in the inception phase, SPC has begun to hire key program staff and refurbish office facilities in preparation. Additionally, Australia has funded an Infrastructure Specialist Unit (ISU) to embed technical infrastructure expertise within the Program Management Office and oversee construction. The ISU has already carried out preparatory works in Kiribati (including site inspection, survey, and removal of UXOs). Although PHWP is a multi-donor initiative, Australia and NZ were the only donors to finalise financial support during the reporting period.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the 2023/24 FY, DFAT entered a grant arrangement with the Pacific Community (SPC), to implement the Pacific Humanitarian Warehousing Program (PHWP). PHWP will support 14 Pacific island countries and Timor-Leste to develop a sovereign, humanitarian warehousing capability to improve their disaster preparedness and resilience. PHWP seeks to strengthen SPC&apos;s capacity in humanitarian assistance and disaster preparedness, in turn responding to the needs articulated in the Boe Declaration for regional organisations to play a greater role in regional security issues, including disasters. To date, PHWP support has provided SPC a platform to expand its technical mandate and co-ordinate and convene Pacific National Disaster Managements Offices to discuss their humanitarian supplies and storage needs, increase SPC staffing numbers, and re-furbish office space.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
